          "heading": "why_in_hospital",
          "source_ids": [
            "sections.0",
            "sections.1"
          ],
          "text": "You were in a road traffic collision as a restrained driver. You were admitted with these injuries:\n\n- A broken left shin bone (closed left tibial shaft fracture)\n- A small tear to your spleen (grade II splenic laceration)\n- Broken ribs on the left side (ribs 7 to 9) with a small collection of blood around your lung (haemothorax)\n- Small fractures to a bone in your lower back (L1 transverse process fractures)"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "c_sections.1",
          "heading": "what_we_did",
          "source_ids": [
            "sections.2",
            "sections.3"
          ],
          "text": "We did scans and X-rays to check all your injuries. Your spine and pelvis were clear on imaging. A repeat scan of your spleen on Day 4 showed it was improving. Your blood count stayed stable throughout your stay.\n\nFor your broken shin bone, you had an operation on 13/05. A metal nail was placed inside the bone to hold it in place.\n\nFor your spleen, no operation was needed. We watched it closely with regular checks and blood tests.\n\nFor the blood around your lung, a chest drain was put in to remove it. The drain was taken out on 15/05. A chest X-ray on Day 4 showed things were satisfactory after the drain was removed.\n\nThe physiotherapy team helped you start moving. You are putting some weight through your left leg, as advised by the orthopaedic team."
        },
        {
          "section_id": "c_sections.2",
          "heading": "medicines",
          "source_ids": [],
          "text": "{{MEDICATIONS}}"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "c_sections.3",
          "heading": "next_steps",
          "source_ids": [
            "sections.7"
          ],
          "text": "You have the following follow-up appointments:\n\n- Orthopaedic fracture clinic in 2 weeks to check your leg.\n- A telephone review with the general surgery team at 6 weeks about your spleen. They will give you advice about a vaccination for your spleen at that call.\n- No further routine follow-up is needed with the respiratory or thoracics team."
        },
        {
